package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setTarkirDragonstorm = StorySection(
    "tdm", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm",
    "The clans of Tarkir won their freedom from the dragonlords with a ritual nobody understood, and the storms it woke began pouring dragons into every world there is.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-the-set", "The Set",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-release", "Release",
                    listOf(
                        "Tarkir Dragonstorm came out on April 11, 2025, under the set code TDM. Players on MTG Arena had it three days earlier, on April 8, and prerelease events ran in stores from April 4 to April 10. It was the hundred and fourth expansion in the history of the game, a set of 287 cards, and the third Standard set of 2025, coming after Aetherdrift and before Final Fantasy. Wizards had first hinted at it nearly two years before, at the Announcement Day panel at Gen Con in August 2023, where the only thing anyone was told was that Magic was going back to Tarkir.",
                        "It arrived with a small family of products around it. The main set was sold in Play Boosters and Collector Boosters. Five Commander decks, one for each clan, carried their own code, TDC, and ten Special Guests cards rode along in the boosters under the code SPG. The prerelease came in five different packs, one per clan, each with a clan booster that held a rare or mythic tied to that clan and a spindown die in the clan's colours, so that a player walked into the event already wearing a side. Stores that sold a full booster box handed out an extended art {Qarsi Revenant} as a buy a box promo, and the bundle held a foil alternate art {Temur Battlecrier}.",
                        "The set's slogan was Fight Dragons With Dragons, and it meant it. Dragons pour out of storms on nearly every page of it, and the clans of Tarkir answer them with dragons of their own, ridden into battle, raised in monasteries and treated as members of the family. The look of the whole set leans on that idea, from basic lands showing the shadow of a dragon passing overhead to a headline card, {Mox Jasper}, that only works if a dragon is already on your side of the table.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-design", "Clans and Dragons",
                    listOf(
                        "The first trip to Tarkir, the Khans of Tarkir block of 2014 and 2015, had been two worlds in one. Khans of Tarkir gave players five clans built in three colour wedges, each clan with its own mechanic, and fans loved it. Then the time travel story of Fate Reforged rewrote history, and Dragons of Tarkir replaced the clans with dragonlords and two colour broods. Players liked the dragons and missed the clans. The design team for the return set out to give them both at once, a world where the clans were back and the dragons had never left, which Wizards called the best of both worlds.",
                        "Vision design was led by Erik Lauer, with Mark Rosewater on the team, and Adam Prosak led set design. Lauer had been the development lead for the original Khans block, and leading the vision for a return to Tarkir had been on his personal bucket list. He did it before he retired in 2024. The result is a three colour set again, with each wedge clan given a fresh mechanic of its own, and with dragons spread across every colour, every rarity and every clan, so that a deck of any shape could fight for the sky.",
                        "The set also leans on the spirit dragon [Ugin](who/ugin), whose old wounds and long sleep on Tarkir are the root of everything that has gone wrong there. His colourless fire, ghostfire, runs through the whole product as a special card frame on ten mythic rares, and his own card, {Ugin, Eye of the Storms}, is one of the set's two planeswalkers. The other belongs to Elspeth Tirel, the archangel who comes to Tarkir looking for answers in the story and leaves it having found far worse ones.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-in-the-story", "Where It Sits",
                    listOf(
                        "Tarkir Dragonstorm is the last chapter of the Dragonstorm Arc, a year long storyline that began in July 2024 with Bloomburrow and ran on through Duskmourn and [Aetherdrift](aetherdrift). That year was itself the middle part of a three year story plan that Wizards had codenamed Metronome. All year the dragons had been leaking into other people's stories. On Bloomburrow a dragon of the Kolaghan brood came through and was turned by that plane's magic into a bird, the beast printed as {Dragonhawk, Fate's Tempest}. In the Duskmourn story a dragon of no clear lineage burned down a forest, a scene shown on a printing of {Scorching Dragonfire}. And at the end of the great race of Aetherdrift, a dragon attacked Ghirapur.",
                        "The set belongs to the age after the Phyrexian [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse), when the doors between worlds called [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) were left hanging open and almost every planeswalker lost the spark that had let them travel alone. Two of Tarkir's own were among those [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost). Narset, now waymaster of the Jeskai, could no longer leave her world, and Sarkhan Vol, who had loved dragons all his life and had been able to become one, lost both the spark and the dragon shape that went with it. What each of them did with that loss is the spine of the story.",
                        "The story itself ran on the Magic Story site from March 3 to March 17, 2025, in seven episodes written by Cassandra Khaw, alongside five shorter tales, one for each clan, by Rhiannon Rasmussen, Michael Yichao, Marcus Terrell Smith, Seanan McGuire and K. Arsenault Rivera. Before it, in February, the game designers Lauren Bond and DK Billins laid out the world and its clans in a two part Planeswalker's Guide. A shorter telling of the main story sits in the Lore section as [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm). This page follows it [episode by episode](tdm-the-waymaster), and the clan tales get [their own tab](tdm-clan-tales).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Collecting Tarkir: Dragonstorm, by Athena Froehlich and Jubilee Finnegan, magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Trivia"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Dragonstorm Arc"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-the-plane", "The Plane",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-a-remade-world", "A Remade World",
                    listOf(
                        "Tarkir is a world soaked in draconic energy, where dragons are not hatched from eggs but born whole out of storms. For as long as anyone can remember, its peoples have lived under that threat, and for centuries the dragons and the clans of humans, orcs, ainok, djinn, naga and others were locked in a balance of power, each hoping to gain the upper hand. The Tarkir of this set is not the first version of the plane, though. It is a world that was rewritten.",
                        "In another history, the clans won. The dragons died out, and five warring khans ruled a plane without them. Then Sarkhan Vol travelled back roughly twelve hundred years, to the moment when [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) was killing his brother [Ugin](who/ugin) the spirit dragon, and Sarkhan saved him. Ugin did not die. He was terribly wounded and fell into a long sleep inside a nest of hedrons on Tarkir, and as he healed, his draconic essence seeped into the soul of the plane and fed the dragonstorms. The storms grew stronger, the dragons grew in number until the clans could not survive them, and history bent around that one rescue.",
                        "Under the five dragonlords, Dromoka, Ojutai, Silumgar, Kolaghan and Atarka, the dragons hunted down the khans and broke the clans. Survivors were allowed to join the dragonlords' broods if they renounced the clans ever existed, and those who refused were wiped out or driven into hiding. For centuries the broods fought one another over land and belief, each trying to be the plane's single master, and over time they grew complacent, sure of their hold on the people beneath them. Life in the broods was hard and empty, and whispers of rebellion never quite died.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-revival", "The Clans Return",
                    listOf(
                        "About five years before the set, Ugin woke and left Tarkir. Without his essence feeding them, the dragonstorms finally eased. Then the Phyrexians came, and the dragons took heavy losses fighting them. Between the weaker storms and the dead, the dragonlords' grip loosened, and the scattered militias and guerrilla bands of the humanoid rebellions grew into open revolt under the banners of the old clan names. The five rebellions found their footing at the same moment, and for the first time in centuries the dragonlords could be beaten.",
                        "Narset, once a favourite student of the dragonlord Ojutai, wanted something more than another war of attrition that would leave Tarkir in ruins. She believed people and dragons each had to find their own place on the plane. In her research she found a worn and faded scroll describing the Stormnexus Ritual, a spell that claimed to call forth the soul of Tarkir. She was afraid of it, and the other rebel leaders talked her into it. At the ruins of the Crucible of the Spirit Dragon, with the dragonlords and their broods closing in, they cast it.",
                        "The ritual raised a dragonstorm larger than any Tarkir had seen, and five dragon shapes came out of it, spirits brimming with power but without form. Narset leapt onto one first and shaped it with her will, and each of the other leaders followed, until five spirit dragons and five new clan leaders were bound together, for better or worse. The battle that followed ended with the dragonlords seemingly swallowed by the storm. The clans that rebuilt afterward were new things, drawing on the old clans and even on the broods they had fought, held together by diplomacy and a fragile peace that often broke into open fighting over land, belief and resources.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The essence of Tarkir was shaped into draconic embodiments of the re-formed clans.",
                        "Call the Spirit Dragons",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-dragonstorms", "Dragonstorms",
                    listOf(
                        "The ritual had a cost nobody saw coming. It upset the balance of draconic mana that Ugin's presence had kept in the storms, and the mana began to mix in unpredictable ways. Dragonstorms grew more frequent and more violent, and they began producing new kinds of dragons that belonged to none of the five old lineages. A storm has no cause anyone can find. Its first stage looks like an ordinary thunderstorm, darkening skies and a crackle in the air, and then a nucleus forms, a knot of draconic energy at its heart, and the dragons come out of it fully grown.",
                        "The land around a storm shapes the storm. Over a lake it may be a forest of giant waterspouts, and in the jungle a vast spinning cyclone, and a dragon born in a blizzard is built for the cold. The overflow of energy has begun to change the land itself, raising rock formations shaped like scales and spiny towers across the plains, opening sinkholes in the deserts and raising the water in the jungles. Narset's own reports, which she admitted might be half rumour, spoke of oxen with reptile eyes and cats growing wings. The Jeskai built devices like the {Dragonstorm Globe} to forecast the storms, and {Boulderborn Dragon} shows the stone of the plane itself taking on a dragon's shape.",
                        "Tarkir's dragons now come in two kinds. Clan dragons are the survivors of the old broods and new dragons that keep their features, and they follow the spirit dragons and live alongside the clans, valuing the freedom, stability and protection clan life gives them. Wild dragons answer to no one. They are apex predators in their own territory, no two look alike, and their breath can be anything from a hail of thorns to a swirling mist. Dragons of either kind range from monsters the size of a hill to creatures the size of a house cat. All of them share a language of their own, and though many can speak the common tongue, most choose not to.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The draconic power that flowed out from the dragonstorms imbued the landscape with draconic features—scales, claws, and appetites.",
                        "Boulderborn Dragon",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Part 1, by Lauren Bond and DK Billins, magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-the-clans", "The Clans",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-abzan", "Abzan",
                    listOf(
                        "The Abzan hold the deserts and steppes of the Shifting Wastes, where the sands move constantly and sinkholes open without warning. Their watchword is to do what is necessary. Family is the centre of everything, and the Abzan see it as their duty to preserve the world for the generations after them, a duty that does not end with death. Their central belief is perennation, that every member must keep the cycle of life and death turning so the clan endures. Under Dromoka all of this had been outlawed. Necromancy was banned, the Kin-Trees that held the spirits of the ancestors were destroyed, and speaking with the dead was forbidden. Defectors like Anafenza challenged the dragonlord and died for it, and during the Phyrexian invasion many in Dromoka's brood called up their ancestors anyway, became heretics, and hid in the desert caverns where the dragon could not reach them.",
                        "Today the clan is led by a khan, Felothar of House Zanhar, chosen for her part in the rebellion and her descent from Reyhan, the last khan before the clans fell, and by a Council of Houses. A house is a great extended family, by blood, marriage or adoption, and five great houses rule. Gudal, the House of Wit, controls the water. Emesh, the House of Courage, forges the weapons. Mevak, the House of Peace, weaves and smiths the armour. Fenzala, the House of Hospitality, runs the trade networks. Zanhar, the House of Grit, is among the oldest, famous for its numbers, its giant Kin-Tree and its dragon hunters. Those adopted into a house are called krumar and are family in every sense. A Kin-Tree is half living wood and half golden spirit, a person joins one by feeding it their blood, and the rare punishment of being cut off from it is the {Kin-Tree Severance} the cards remember.",
                        "The Abzan fight slowly and patiently, behind walls and heavy cavalry, outlasting enemies until they break, and the elite Yathan serve as their scouts, assassins and best soldiers. They are traders above all, running caravans along the Salt Road for every clan, and poets who embroider verses into their clothes and etch them into their weapons. Their great city, {Great Arashin City}, was once Dromoka's Great Aerie and can raise walls against the storms. Qatros Karst, a newer city, was carved out of the rebels' old hiding caves after a sinking revealed it. Abzan clan dragons are large, steady and mild tempered, chosen as much for temperament as for strength, and they breathe a searing light that can strip flesh in moments.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Abzan believe in perennation—death is not only an end, but a return to the beginning.",
                        "Perennation",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-jeskai", "Jeskai",
                    listOf(
                        "The Jeskai live in the cold Tiansun mountains in the east, among volcanic peaks and great lakes, and their saying is that they act as one. They follow the Way, which seeks a unity of thought and a shared understanding of reality, a world-mind that thinks and feels as one and so ends suffering. Its guiding mantra, to achieve peace, achieve unity, was found in the writings of Shu Yun in a hidden archive that survived Ojutai's purges. Under Ojutai, anyone who questioned the dragonlord's teaching was killed or exiled, and the exiles traded true history in secret until the Phyrexian invasion, when they flocked to Narset, Ojutai's former protégé, and she took up the leadership. The Jeskai rejected Ojutai's teaching of draconic rebirth and care little for the afterlife, which puts them at odds with the other clans.",
                        "The Way has three principles, and each of the three great monasteries follows one. Cori Mountain Monastery, built in the flooded caldera of a volcano among red mineral-fed plants, teaches cooperative action, that unity of mind comes through unity of body. Dirgur Lake Monastery, on a floating island near the Salt Road, teaches undivided thought, and its monks are masters of persuasion and politics. The Storm Crane Monastery teaches the aligned heart, helping those who are struggling so they can think of more than survival, and it is a ship, built from the ruins of the old Sage-Eye sanctuary destroyed in the invasion, that sails the lakes and rivers and can rise into the air. The stewards of the three monasteries form the clan's council, and one of them is chosen waymaster.",
                        "Monks leave their families and their possessions behind and spend their days in meditation, martial training, study and magic, on the path of the warrior or the scholar. Skyriders have revived the old mantis riders and also raise the clan dragons, which are chosen for intelligence and calm and breathe a cold so fierce it freezes enemies where they stand. Villagers grow rice, wheat, lotus and tea, leave gifts at roadside shrines for strangers in need, and play a ball game called orjsu. Great bells in the monasteries warn of coming storms. In the cards, {Cori Mountain Monastery} is one of the set's rare lands, and {Rally the Monastery} shows the monks turning out to defend their home.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Each step we take is a decision that changes the course of our futures. We must be careful where we tread.",
                        "Narset, Jeskai Waymaster",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-sultai", "Sultai",
                    listOf(
                        "The Sultai live in the jungles and swamps of a vast river delta in the south, and their saying is that nothing goes to waste. They turn the jungle into rich farms, build beautiful cities among its dangers, and raise their honoured dead to keep leading and serving. Under Silumgar, a person's worth depended on the whim of a bored and paranoid dragon, and the rebels saw his rule and the decadent old Sultai lords as the same cruelty. They reached back to the clan's origins from before its ancient pacts with demons, many of their mages were trained by naga who had turned against the dragon, and their necromancers raised fallen warriors and leaders to swell their small numbers.",
                        "Because of that history, power among the Sultai is spread out. The Fangkeeper rules, but mostly manages relations between villages, the standing army and the defences, and sits in judgment beside the spirit dragon. Villages elect mayors who govern with a lead necromancer priest, and many have a naga protector who blesses and curses as justice demands. The army, the Lasyd, is small and elite, full of scouts, spies and assassins, and merchants called the Panjasi double as the clan's eyes abroad. Rakshasa demons are shunned, and those who bargain with them are exiled or killed, remembered as the enablers of Silumgar and of the traitor Tasigur.",
                        "The Sultai believe in the Abiding Harvest, that every living thing dies and its essence returns to the land to feed the next harvest. Raising the dead is done through the Rite of Renewal, and it is now an honour, given to beloved leaders, artisans and warriors who offered themselves, and raising anyone unwilling is outlawed. The risen are called sibsig. They keep their memories, often with gold or jade prosthetics, and are revered, though some come back different. Sultai dragons are lithe ambush hunters with choking poison breath, and some are chosen simply for beauty. In the cards, {The Sibsig Ceremony} shows the rite, and {Kishla Village} is the stilt village rising over flooded ruins as the waters climb.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Once flesh, now gold and jade. Once dead, now alive again. Once respected, now honored beyond imagination.",
                        "The Sibsig Ceremony",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-mardu", "Mardu",
                    listOf(
                        "The Mardu are nomads of the central steppe, and their saying is dare to keep up. They are known for lightning-wielding fighters and archers, expert beastriders, fierce loyalty to the clan and the finest blacksmiths on Tarkir. Under Kolaghan they were always moving and always at war, and during the Phyrexian invasion her brood fought on the front lines and suffered the heaviest losses of any. As soon as the invaders were gone, rebel leaders raised the Mardu banner and went to war with Kolaghan before either side could recover, gathering recruits from every brood until they were an army.",
                        "A khan leads them with the Dalkovan Assembly, a council of generals, each of whom leads a travelling camp city called a dalkovan that can be built in less than a day. Civilian and soldier blur together, and a hardened warrior may also be an expert cook or tailor. The army has infantry, cavalry that makes up most of its strength, and a small corps of siege engineers. Since the old Edicts of Ilagra were lost, the clan lives by a new code, the Decree of Thunder, whose three tenets are that loyalty is earned, community is strength, and victory is survival. The Mardu never invade without first sending a diplomat, and those who surrender are treated with respect and offered a place in the clan.",
                        "Their belief is the Clash of Sky and Ember, two forces of sky and land, the Skybreath and the Emberheart, forever in conflict in lightning, wind and earthquake, and their stormsingers can briefly bend those forces. A Mardu has a family name, a chosen name taken after the Lightning Proving, when the young climb a mountain to catch lightning in a storm, and sometimes a third name given by the khan for a great deed. Mardu dragons are fast and breathe lightning. {Dalkovan Encampment} is one of the set's lands, and {Voice of Victory} sings the clan into battle.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Come crackling spear, come beating wing! Come cavalier, come taut bowstring!",
                        "Voice of Victory",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-temur", "Temur",
                    listOf(
                        "The Temur live in the snowy Qal Sisma mountains of the north, semi-nomads who winter in villages and roam in summer camps, and their saying is that the land will guide them. Under Atarka the people starved, hunted by brood dragons as prey, and every scrap of meat went to the dragons before it reached a fire. But many of those dragons had grown lazy, and the rebels knew the land well enough to evade even Atarka. Whisperers came out of hiding to speak of the Temur and the future they saw, and with hunters at their head the rebellions brought the dragonlord down.",
                        "Two leaders share the clan. The Dragonclaw is a revered warrior chosen by the people, and the Twice Whisperer is chosen by the spirits from among all the whisperers. The first handles the tangible world and the second the spiritual one, and the clan's small family groups mirror the same pairing. Whisperers heal, protect and foresee but do not hunt or fight, and their knowledge lets the Temur predict the dragonstorms better than any other clan. Each Temur bonds with a single animal companion who is part of the family, and wrestling, storytelling and small portable crafts fill the long winters.",
                        "The Temur believe in the Endless Song, the energy in the land that connects all beings past, present and future, and in spirits who exist outside time, in pasts that never happened and futures that may never come. They mark the year with Frostsweep in the fall, Thawflow in spring, the Unbroken Night and the Zenith Day. Their dragons are stocky fire-breathers that gather in family-like groups and guard the borders fiercely. {Mistrise Village} is their steam-heated settlement on a field of vents, and the great Rainveil Forest, Glintglaze Lake and the sacred Eternal Ice are all Temur country.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Built on a fumarole field, the village is a refuge of warmth amid the ice of the Qal Sisma mountains.",
                        "Mistrise Village",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Parts 1 and 2, by Lauren Bond and DK Billins, magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-clan-tales", "Clan Tales",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-siege-blossoms", "Siege Blossoms",
                    listOf(
                        "The Abzan tale, by Rhiannon Rasmussen, takes place in Qatros Karst under a long Sultai siege. The Sultai wanted mining rights to the jewel and salt rich cliffs the storms had changed, and their necromancy reached deep into the city at night. Lieutenant Mehtma of House Fenzala, a krumar orphan adopted into the house, was stuck teaching children instead of serving the khan, and every letter she wrote asking for a post went unanswered. Her house's Kin-Tree was young, planted only two years earlier from a cutting of Anafenza's secret grove, and its ancestors had not yet appeared in it.",
                        "Her rival, Captain Jurjis of House Agach, a house that had been refused a Kin-Tree, seized the cows the khan herself had given her. While she chased them, a fellow krumar named Cemil, the warden's assistant, revealed that he had always thought of himself as Sultai and fled over the walls into the Sultai fog. He had been the distraction. Jurjis, who had arranged the meeting, used the passphrase Cemil gave him to reach the tree, killed a young loxodon named Enti who had just been accepted into the house, and meant to take a cutting or cut the tree down.",
                        "Mehtma fought him wounded, and as she weakened, the tree glowed and Enti stepped out of it as a golden ancestor spirit, the first the young Kin-Tree had ever held, and lent her the strength to win. Mehtma killed the traitor with Cemil's Sultai sickle, and a single golden flower opened on the tree. Searching Jurjis's office, she found every one of her letters and every damage report, never sent, and the requisitions likely skimmed for his house or for the Sultai. She traded silence for the post of captain of logistics, promised to get word of the siege to the khan, and finally got cream in her coffee.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-unknown-way", "The Unknown Way",
                    listOf(
                        "The Jeskai tale, by Michael Yichao, follows Asham, a young monk of Cori Mountain Monastery. Chosen as his class's best disciple, he had been beaten in a single strike by a wandering Temur fighter who had asked the monastery for a sparring match, and he took it as a disgrace to himself, his monastery and the whole clan. Alone by the caldera lake, he tried and failed again and again to master a final form that should conjure dragonfire from his hands. Then his childhood friend Ru, an ainok farmer who had turned down the same invitation to the monastery to stay with his family, came to water his ox.",
                        "Ru listened, teased him and told him there was more to the Way than fighting, that a loss is no dishonour, and that the farmers who grow the monks' food serve the Jeskai too. Asham's form needed him to release the self, and when Ru asked to be taught instead of beaten, Asham finally found the flame. He went after the Temur wanderer, Ai Wen, an elder of the Sahn Ni family, and demanded a rematch. She told him a bout between an elder and a disciple was never a fair match, threw him into the bushes, and when he came back with hands on fire, slammed him into the stone steps and broke his ribs, without ever drawing the great sword at her side.",
                        "She praised his breakthrough before she left him. Asham woke in his home village with Ru tending him. Ru had told the monastery he fell down the mountain, and everyone believed it at once. Asham admitted that joining the monastery had been a way of running from his feelings and his family, and said he wanted to train at the other monasteries to understand the world and the other clans. Ru kissed him, then went to wash the bandages, and told him he would be there when he came back.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-betrayal", "Betrayal",
                    listOf(
                        "The Sultai tale, by Marcus Terrell Smith, is the darkest of the five. Nishang had been one of Silumgar's soldiers, who once marched into villages, took prisoners and was promised sibsig servants of his own. Now he was dying of a wasting disease, drinking alone in Kheru City and raging that the new order had made the sibsig honoured instead of enslaved. On the swamp a glowing carp led his boat to a ghostly forest, where he died and woke again, rebuilt with a golden arm, a golden eye and a new jaw, before his old comrade Titsui and Sidisi, Silumgar's undead vizier.",
                        "They gave him a mission. At a ceremony in Qarsi Palace, Dauna, chief necromancer of the Sultai, would welcome hundreds of newly risen sibsig into the clan in the sacred pool of an ancient tree, before Teval herself, and Nishang's arm carried poison to destroy them all. On his way, people crowned him with flowers and a woman kissed him, and for the first time in his life he apologised, and helped a fallen boy to his feet. Then he realised Titsui was lying about how he had died. Titsui was a rakshasa in disguise, who could also wear Sidisi's shape.",
                        "Nishang refused the mission, but he had given up a piece of his soul to seal the pact, and the rakshasa marched his body to the ceremony anyway, smiling. Inside the pool, the poison melted the gathered sibsig away while he watched, unable to close his eyes. Dauna speared him and dragged him into the air in horror. He told her he had changed, and maybe he had, but part of his soul would always belong to Silumgar. The tale ends there, with a massacre, a demon unpunished and a plot to restore Sidisi still alive in the swamps.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Evil wears many faces. Keep your windows closed at night, or it might wear yours.",
                        "Sultai warning, Qarsi Revenant",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-lightning-proving", "Lightning Proving",
                    listOf(
                        "The Mardu tale, by Seanan McGuire, belongs to Paala. As a small child she had fled a burning caravan camp on the Salt Road with her mother, a former Abzan diplomat, and her baby sister Refia, escaping assassins sent over a feud in her father's family, who was almost certainly killed. A Mardu hunting party found them starving on the steppe ahead of a dragonstorm and took them in. The family had tended the clan's great riding cats, the manul, ever since, and Paala had grown up among their kittens.",
                        "Now she was of age to take the Lightning Proving, and some of the young Mardu, led by a hunter's son named Altan, said she would never be one of them because she had not been born to the clan. On the ride to the mountain Altan attacked her and fell with his riding bird down a slope, and she dug him out of the rocks though he told her he hated her, because it was the Mardu thing to do. An archer shot at her and Paala took the bow. She marked a nest of vipers with arrows so no one behind her would step in it, and at the summit another girl, Emina, attacked her with a sling.",
                        "In the storm the lightning looked to Paala like a nest of vipers, and she understood that only someone with true love for the clan could take it. She held out her hand, the largest bolt came to her, and the world went white. She woke tied to her saddle and thought she had failed, but Emina, Altan and the archer had brought her down themselves, and told the elders she had beaten them, helped them and reached the summit first. She took the name Paala, clan's aid, and named her cat Lightning.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-together", "Together",
                    listOf(
                        "The Temur tale, by K. Arsenault Rivera, begins with the spirit dragon Ureni watching threads of cruel blue light bind a sleeping Temur camp until everyone in it was dead with their eyes open. Eshki Dragonclaw was winning a wrestling match at a festival when Alniul, the Twice Whisperer, brought a hunter carrying a stag veined with the same blue. Ureni told them the answer could not be given, only learned, and that they had two weeks. Alniul climbed alone to the spirits and came back wounded in spirit, with a vision of Rainveil Forest.",
                        "Their journey there took longer because Eshki would not leave grieving camps behind, and threw into the snow anyone who said the sick were weak and should be left to die. In the silent heart of Rainveil they found a lake of blue light, and in it Surrak, the last huntcaller, who had led the Temur before Eshki and served Atarka. Eshki had beaten him in front of the army, driving a shard of ice into his chest, and the wound had never healed. It was the source of the blight, and Surrak was bound to the earth by its threads, still telling her the weak should be left behind.",
                        "Alniul sang a calling and the spirits of everyone who had died in the forest came, among them those who had done terrible things to survive Atarka, stolen food, killed to eat and lived on things no one should eat. Alniul asked if Eshki would welcome them to the fire. She saw that in casting Surrak aside she had cast out all of them too, and she sang that all were welcome, then pulled Surrak up to sing beside her. They sang until dawn, and a reindeer walked into the clearing. Months later the three of them shared a meal, Surrak swore he would take the clan back one day, and Eshki poured him a drink.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Twice Whisperer heals. The Dragonclaw protects. Together, we will rebuild the Temur.",
                        "Eshki Dragonclaw",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Abzan: Siege Blossoms, by Rhiannon Rasmussen, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Jeskai: The Unknown Way, by Michael Yichao, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Sultai: Betrayal, by Marcus Terrell Smith, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Mardu: Where Lightning Tells Our Story, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Temur: Together Survives the Pack, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-the-waymaster", "The Waymaster",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-stories-and-bones", "Stories and Bones",
                    listOf(
                        "The story opens in a crowded tavern on the edge of the fishing village around Dirgur Lake Monastery, the first or last stop for anyone on the Salt Road. Narset, waymaster of the Jeskai and by now in practice the clan's leader, liked it because almost no one treated her as a ruler there. That night a pair of travelling performers in headdresses full of hidden masks put on a show of the revolution. One of them appeared in the face of Ojutai, and then, to Narset's horror, in her own, rendered serene and empty as a death mask.",
                        "The crowd would not let them tell it. A Temur woman shouted that Narset had to be talked into helping, a young Mardu engineer said she had only found the ritual, and a Sultai noble with a jaw of carved white jade, risen from death herself, said Kotis was the real hero as the only leader who had died and come back. The Sultai woman told the young Mardu how she had died, stabbed on the Salt Road by a starving Mardu boy she had just fed, for two days of rice, and the engineer could only apologise. Illusions of Silumgar, Atarka, Kolaghan, Dromoka and Ojutai rose out of the smoke, and the performers swapped masks to show the rebel leaders, Eshki and Alniul, Zurgo, Felothar, Kotis and Narset.",
                        "Narset watched it remembering how much she had loved Ojutai, and how he had made his path seem the only path. She respected Shiko, the spirit dragon she had shaped with her own reverence for truth, but she did not love Shiko the way she had loved her teacher. When the audience started arguing over whether the dragons chose the leaders or took a piece of their souls, she finally spoke up, quietly telling what had really happened at the Crucible and adding that the worst was still to come. Then a man burst in shouting for the waymaster. A woman with wings and glowing eyes had arrived and wanted to see her. Narset knew her name. Elspeth.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-omens", "Omens",
                    listOf(
                        "Elspeth Tirel had become an archangel, and it had cost her much of what made her human. Aboard the Storm Crane Monastery a child asked whether she was a dragon. She told Narset that dragonstorms were appearing across the Multiverse and asked how Tarkir kept them at bay. Narset had to answer that it did not. She showed Elspeth her annotated map of storms raising water, opening sinkholes and growing scales on the land, and said something as powerful as [Ugin](who/ugin) was at work. Ugin was gone and [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) was believed dead, and she was sure the storms had never grown this strong while Ugin was on Tarkir. She would not use the ritual again. She feared it had already broken something.",
                        "Then the alarm bells rang a message the monks had only ever practised. A wild dragon had chewed up through the hull of the flying ship into the school in its belly. Teachers stood between it and the students. One girl held a barrier across a collapsed classroom with five younger children behind her as the dragon drew breath to freeze them. On Narset's signal Elspeth dove straight into its mouth and drove her sword into the roof of it, the monks beat at it from every side, and the children were passed out one by one. The dragon fled, and Elspeth came out red from head to foot.",
                        "That night Taigam, Narset's old rival from her days with Ojutai, came to her study. He had stayed loyal to the dragonlord until he was forced to renounce him, and he still missed Ojutai as she did. He told her the angel was an omen, that wild dragons had never attacked like this before she came, and that the waymaster's duty was to her own people. At dawn Narset told Elspeth the Jeskai could not help her. Then she settled a pack on her shoulder and said that she could.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-crucible", "The Crucible",
                    listOf(
                        "Far to the south, Ajani Goldmane hunted gazelle alone near the Abzan border. He had been compleated in the invasion and had served [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) gladly, as he saw it, raising his axe against his own friends, and he could not forgive himself. He had promised the Abzan khan he would help guard the borders if they left him alone. The clan sent ambassador after ambassador to draw him back into the world, and after he refused the nineteenth, a djinni named Nur, known as the Wandering Warden, began turning up at his hut with far too much food. She soothed an ancestral maelstrom of lost Abzan spirits while he watched, and told him that what he was living was not life but death deferred.",
                        "Narset and Elspeth flew on a young Jeskai dragon to the Crucible of the Spirit Dragon, where Narset had fought the dragonlords. In a rift below floated hedrons, the stones [Nahiri](who/nahiri) once made on Zendikar, which Narset said Ugin taught her to shape, and which held part of his life force. A voice spoke out of them, telling them to come to the temple in the heart of the storms where the truth awaited. Narset knew of no such temple. Then Sarkhan Vol walked out of the wasteland, scarred, bearded and burning with fever, and told them Tarkir wanted another storm and that the whole Multiverse would become a realm of dragons.",
                        "When Elspeth said that would not happen while she lived, Sarkhan said that could be fixed and attacked. She punched him in the ribs, and when he rose to stab her in the back, Narset blasted him across the ground. Elspeth told him that if he attacked them again she would kill him. Sarkhan had lost his spark, and with it, nearly, his dragon form, which now hurt to take. He limped for hours to his hut on the cold edge of Mardu land, too ashamed to go home. A hooded man was waiting there. His name was Taigam, and he said he had something Sarkhan wanted.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "With a swift motion Narset struck Sarkhan down. She would not let his fevered dreams of draconic dominance come to fruition. Not after all she had done to free Tarkir.",
                        "Narset's Rebuke",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Episodes 1 to 3, by Cassandra Khaw, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Dragonstorm Arc"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-heart-of-fire", "Heart of Fire",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-dragons-heart", "The Dragon's Heart",
                    listOf(
                        "Sarkhan felt old, brittle and rotting from the inside, as he had once felt under [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas). Taigam promised him a way to fly with a dragon's wings again. They needed a young dragon with a strong heart, one that could bear pain to match what Sarkhan had suffered. They found one on the steppe, newly born and playing with a dead gazelle like a kitten with yarn, and Sarkhan was almost glad of the danger. If the dragon killed him, that would be a better end than slowly withering. He went at it with his spear and tore open its throat, and Taigam pinned it to the ground in chains of blue fire.",
                        "For a moment Sarkhan faltered. He had loved and worshipped dragons all his life, and this was blasphemy against everything he had held holy. He told himself the strong endure and the weak perish, and that if a dragon could die to someone like him it did not deserve its heart. He cut it open and took hold of the heart while Taigam chanted, and as he chanted with him, Sarkhan recognised that the spell was corrupted and thought, for the first and last time, that they had made a mistake. Then he bit into the heart, and his world became fire.",
                        "The spell was the Stormnexus Ritual, and it gave him more than he had bargained for. His dragon body came back larger and more terrible than before, and every wild dragon the storms produced now answered his call. Ajani was hunting with Abzan riders near the Mardu border when a wave of magic flattened the grass. The gazelle herd broke its own necks fleeing, and the Abzan dragons threw their riders, one biting his rider in half. A red dragon of impossible size rose into the sky with dozens more around it, and Ajani knew it at once as Sarkhan, changed into something like a raging inferno.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "With Taigam whispering encouragement in his ear, Sarkhan threw aside his misgivings. The ritual would return him to his former glory. All he needed was a dragon's heart.",
                        "Sarkhan's Resolve",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-council", "The Council of Houses",
                    listOf(
                        "Narset and Elspeth had gone looking for the temple in the stormplains, where the storms were fiercest, and Abzan outriders arrested them for trespassing. Narset insisted the clans' accord allowed them to be there, then had to admit the khans had never actually agreed on that accord. She asked Elspeth for patience, because breaking out could start a war between the Jeskai and the Abzan, and Elspeth agreed to give her one hour. The cell was very comfortable, with carpets, silk cots and a plate of honeyed pastries Narset could not stop eating. While they waited, she explained that the Abzan think the Jeskai dogmatic and arrogant, and that she was not sure they were wrong.",
                        "Felothar received them before her whole Council of Houses, and it went as Narset had feared. The old woman of House Emesh wanted to know why a khan of another clan had entered Abzan land unannounced. Fenzala and Mevak wanted to discuss trade, including a red mineral that had appeared in Jeskai lands. Someone demanded Elspeth be examined to make sure she was not a dragon. The young girl standing in for the dying head of House Gudal wondered whether Zanhar's records held anything about a voice in the Crucible. The djinni of House Zanhar asked why they would look in the stormplains when storms happen everywhere.",
                        "Elspeth announced that the hour was up. Then the doors crashed open and Ajani stormed in to tell the khan that Sarkhan Vol had taken control of the wild dragons on the border. For the first time since she had come to Tarkir, Elspeth's face showed real feeling. Ajani had last seen her when he tried to kill her for Phyrexia. She told him to leave the past with the dead, that he did not have to stay there with them. He laughed and said he was afraid he must. Then Felothar cleared her throat and asked to hear more about Sarkhan and the dragons.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Elspeth saw their capture as an inconvenience, costing them precious time. Narset saw it as an opportunity to recruit allies to their cause.",
                        "Stormplain Detainment",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-temple", "The Temple in the Storm",
                    listOf(
                        "Ajani begged Felothar to let his friends go without the council's consensus. She agreed, and turned to preparing Arashin's walls, which would take a day to raise. Narset warned that walls would not be enough, that Sarkhan would bring not dozens or hundreds of dragons to the city but thousands. Nur arrived to back her up, reporting that her scouts had already counted hundreds. Felothar still put her people first, but provisioned the three of them with whatever they needed. Alone in a guest room, Ajani insisted that he had served Phyrexia willingly. Elspeth told him he had been forgiven before their fight even began. Narset asked him to come with them and help stop the storms. He said he could not, and left.",
                        "Elspeth and Narset walked into the storm itself, wrapped in a veil of Narset's blue magic, past dragons still shaking their wings dry from their birth, toward a knife of light at its heart. They came out into silence at the foot of an enormous ruined temple that appeared in none of Ojutai's histories or Narset's studies. Deep beneath it was an Omenpath unlike any they knew, ragged at the edges like a bite torn out of the world. Narset stepped through without hesitation, and Elspeth followed.",
                        "On the other side lay the Meditation Realm. Elspeth saw endless water and strange spiralling rock, and Narset saw a shattering maze of stairs and roads, some of which seemed to bend through the past into the present. Both felt the place was alive and that what they saw was meant to distract them. Then they found someone standing on white sand, older and more tired than they remembered, with a strange furred creature asleep in his arms. It was [Jace Beleren](who/jace). Elspeth had last seen him with her sword through his chest, compleated. He said he had resisted phyresis by force of will and that he was there to fix his mistakes.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Meditation Realm is a willful puzzle. Seek, and it will impede. Wait, and it will permit passage.",
                        "Stillness in Motion",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Episodes 4 and 5, by Cassandra Khaw, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Dragonstorm Arc"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-ghostfire", "Ghostfire",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-how-wretched-love", "How Wretched Love",
                    listOf(
                        "The creature in Jace's arms was Loot, and his mind held a map of the whole Multiverse, every Omenpath and every plane as it was born and as it died. Some time before, in an inn in Sultai country where the coffee came sweet with cardamom and cinnamon, Vraska had begged [Jace](who/jace) to stop. Loot kept trying to hide from him, and she accused him of nearly lobotomising the child. She had lost her spark, and the Phyrexians had changed her forever. She told him they had each other and an odd little family, and asked whether that was not enough. He said it was not, that Phyrexia had broken the Multiverse and he could fix it.",
                        "Jace had followed Loot's map across the Omenpaths to Tarkir, meaning to reach the Meditation Realm. When Loot crawled into a dragonstorm in the Abzan deserts to get away from him, Jace wrapped the child in blue magic and put him under. In the realm, he told Narset and Elspeth that it was no use, that there were no real roads there. Narset understood that their own thoughts were trapping them. She asked them to clear their minds and wait, and when they did, the realm rebuilt itself around something else's will.",
                        "Two great dragons lay twined there, horns and brows touching. One was [Ugin](who/ugin), dull and threadbare. The other was [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), alive. Ugin told them that he and his brother had both cheated death before, so at the end of the [War of the Spark](bolas-war-of-the-spark) he had made this place their shared prison, meant to hold until Bolas died millennia from now. Bolas had no memories, but he felt he knew them, and he felt that Jace had once been his accomplice. Narset begged Ugin for help with the storms. He said everything left of him was spent keeping his brother caged, and that even talking to them put the prison at risk.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-shattering", "The Shattering",
                    listOf(
                        "Bolas was delighted to learn his name. As Ugin roared at them to leave, the Jace beside them melted away, an illusion, and the real Jace appeared beside the great gem between Ugin's horns and closed his hands on it. Light exploded out of it. Elspeth flew at him and swung, but the realm itself moved him out of reach, again and again. Jace told them he did not want to free Bolas. Loot's mind had shown him that the Meditation Realm touched every world and could be shaped by will alone, and with it he could undo the deaths of their friends, make Elspeth human again and set the dragonstorms right.",
                        "Narset pointed out that he did not even know how the storms worked. Loot screamed on the sand, and she picked him up and held him like any frightened Jeskai child. Then Vraska stepped out of hiding and asked Jace to stop, saying there was no going back, only their future together. When Elspeth said killing him might still prevent disaster, Vraska swore she would end anyone who touched him. Jace wanted most of all to save Vraska from what the Phyrexians had done to her.",
                        "For a moment the realm breathed in and Narset saw all the futures they had lost, dead friends alive, worlds unbroken, and at the centre of Jace's wish, Vraska happy and whole. But for all the power he had stolen, Jace was only human. It burned through him like a wick. The horizon broke into mirrored shards over a pouring void that unmade everything it touched, and as it rolled over him, Jace shattered like glass. Elspeth shouted for them to run, and Narset ran with Loot wailing in her arms.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-last-stand", "The Last Stand",
                    listOf(
                        "Behind them Bolas rose laughing out of his broken chains into the emptiness, Ugin threw himself at him and failed to hold him, and both vanished into the void. Elspeth carried Narset through the Omenpath, and they came out under a sky black with dragons. Sarkhan had come to destroy the temple and its Omenpath, the only power on Tarkir that could rival his own. The dragon heart in his chest screamed at him night and day, and he did not care. Vraska vanished with Loot under a camouflage spell. Elspeth meant to face Sarkhan alone. Narset refused, and told her that we do not choose how our stories begin or end, only how we live them, words a friend had once told her.",
                        "Elspeth flew up to buy time, expecting to die. Narset stood alone on the temple platform and fought dragons with her bare hands, turning their own breath and momentum against them until she found again the deep quiet she had known studying under Ojutai. Above, Ajani rode in behind Felothar, saw a dragon of oily shadow creeping up on Narset, and decided, at last, that he could simply choose to do something. He leapt from dragonback and cut off its head. Behind him came the dragons of the Abzan, fighting as one beast with the tactics of centuries of ancestors.",
                        "Elspeth threw herself into Sarkhan's mouth to take his eye and was caught in his jaws. Ajani called out to Sarkhan as his friend, reminding him who he had once been, and for a moment the dragon hesitated. Then Narset asked him what he had cut out of himself to corrupt the ritual, and whose life he had given, and Sarkhan's shame turned to fury. He said the Multiverse deserved to burn for what it had taken from him and lunged for Elspeth, and Ajani caught her as she fell. He told her he had not come to make up for her past deaths but to fight beside her now.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Abzan forces rallied to the call, driving back Sarkhan's feral dragon horde.",
                        "United Battlefront",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-enough", "Enough",
                    listOf(
                        "The Abzan dragons began to die. Six, then eight, then twelve, and the wild dragons, multiplying out of the clouds, began to enjoy it. Narset, Elspeth and Ajani agreed to go for Sarkhan's eyes, knowing what it would cost, and Narset offered to lead because she could read his movements best. Her borrowed Abzan dragon refused her offer to let it escape, saying it would never hear the end of it from the ancestors. Then a single word filled their bones. Enough.",
                        "Ugin came out of the broken realm bloodied and exhausted, and the wild dragons fled shrieking. Sarkhan screamed that Ugin had tormented him enough and poured fire over him, and the flames washed over the spirit dragon like water. Ugin looked at him with a sadness that made Narset ashamed, then opened his mouth, and ghostfire poured out, colourless and without heat. Sarkhan's scales burned to char, and he dove away through the storm, alive.",
                        "Ugin admitted, with a tired laugh, that the storms behaved like whelps when he was not there to keep them in line. Then he told them the rest. He had failed to keep his brother, and Nicol Bolas was free. The story's last scene shows only a pool of silver hanging over nothing and, forming beneath its surface, the silhouette of a blue cloak. A shorter telling of the whole arc is in the Lore section as [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Episodes 6 and 7, by Cassandra Khaw, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Dragonstorm Arc"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-characters", "Characters",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-khans", "The Khans",
                    listOf(
                        "Each clan came out of the revolution with a new leader. Felothar of House Zanhar leads the Abzan. She was a soldier before she was a khan and still dresses like one, and she caused a small scandal by refusing the ceremonial armour made for her and keeping the armour she already had. She gives every house's voice equal weight, which makes her loved and makes every decision take hours. Her card {Felothar, Dawn of the Abzan} rewards sacrifice with counters for every creature, and her battle cry is on it. Narset leads the Jeskai as waymaster, a role she never wanted, and {Narset, Jeskai Waymaster} lets a player throw away a hand and draw for every spell cast that turn.",
                        "Kotis, the Fangkeeper, rules the Sultai, and is remembered as the only rebel leader who died and returned to keep doing the job. {Kotis, the Fangkeeper} is indestructible and casts spells from an opponent's library after hitting them. Zurgo leads the Mardu, and the masks in the tavern called him Zurgo Stormrender. His card {Zurgo, Thunder's Decree} brings warrior tokens with every attack. On his flavour text for {Smile at Death}, he credits an earlier khan, Alesha, with the clan's survival.",
                        "Eshki Dragonclaw leads the Temur with Alniul, the Twice Whisperer, Eshki the body and Alniul the heart, the brash and the thoughtful, as their own story puts it. Eshki won her title by beating the old huntcaller Surrak in front of the army, and she has a habit of throwing into the snow anyone who talks about leaving the weak behind. Alniul is the whisperer who found the Stormnexus Ritual with Narset and foretold Eshki's victory. {Eshki Dragonclaw} grows whenever a player casts both a creature spell and a noncreature spell in a turn, and the set's {Craterhoof Behemoth} reprint carries a line of hers about wishing she were fighting a dragon.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Through perennation, we stand together, families—ancestor and kin─united as one clan! Abzan!",
                        "Felothar, Dawn of the Abzan",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-spirit-dragons", "The Spirit Dragons",
                    listOf(
                        "The five spirit dragons were born from the Stormnexus Ritual and shaped by the hopes of the people who rode them out of the storm. Betor of the Abzan, kin to all, is the lost spirits of the forbidden dead brought together into one form, brilliant as the desert sun and in open defiance of Dromoka's decrees. {Betor, Kin to All} rewards an army of great toughness, drawing cards, untapping everything and finally taking half of each opponent's life. Shiko of the Jeskai was shaped by Narset's reverence for truth and even her secret wish to be more decisive, and her wings look like unrolling scrolls. {Shiko, Paragon of the Way} casts a cheap spell again from the graveyard for free.",
                        "Teval of the Sultai is the judge of the clan, fair and ruthlessly impartial, with gold-tipped scales, and to the Sultai that impartiality was a relief after centuries of Silumgar's whims. {Teval, Arbiter of Virtue} gives every spell delve but costs life with each one. Neriv of the Mardu is the living banner the horde rallies around, his scales streaked with lightning, bringing together a clan that Kolaghan had split. {Neriv, Heart of the Storm} doubles the damage of anything that just arrived.",
                        "Ureni of the Temur is zealous and protective, a dragon who sees the threads of fate, gives the clan clear water in its grove, and will not hand out answers that must be learned. Narset thinks of Ureni as zealous, a dragon who would never let anything happen to the Temur again. {Ureni, the Song Unending} costs eight mana, has protection from white and from black, and spreads damage across the board equal to the lands its owner controls. The card {Call the Spirit Dragons} shows the five taking form, and any player who can put counters on five dragons of five colours with it wins the game.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-old-names", "Old Names",
                    listOf(
                        "A cycle of single coloured legends brings back names from Tarkir's past, some of them changed by the new history. Anafenza is a spirit now. She defied Dromoka and was killed for it, and the Abzan honour her as an early defector, with the first Kin-Tree recovered after the dragonlord's fall named for her. {Anafenza, Unyielding Lineage} grows or makes spirits whenever another creature dies. Taigam, Narset's old rival, is the monk who handed Sarkhan the ritual, and {Taigam, Master Opportunist} copies a second spell and holds the original back for later, much as he waited for his moment.",
                        "Sidisi was Silumgar's vizier, an undead naga who saw her time in power end when the rebels broke into Qarsi Palace. In the Sultai tale, someone wearing her shape is still plotting in the swamps. {Sidisi, Regent of the Mire} trades a creature for a slightly bigger one from the graveyard. Tersa Lightshatter is a legend to the Mardu, someone who climbed for the storm when there were no rules and no certainty of survival, and the young still measure themselves against her at the Lightning Proving. {Tersa Lightshatter} rummages through the graveyard to keep attacking.",
                        "Surrak was the Temur's huntcaller under Atarka, the last man to lead the clan before Eshki, and he spent his life feeding a dragon's hunger. He still believes the strong should survive and the weak be left, and he still means to take his clan back. {Surrak, Elusive Hunter} cannot be countered and draws a card whenever an opponent targets his side of the table.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I've spent my life feeding Atarka's desire and hungers. It's time to see to my own.",
                        "Surrak, Elusive Hunter",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-outsiders", "Walkers and Angels",
                    listOf(
                        "Elspeth Tirel came back from carrying the detonating Filigree Sylex into the Blind Eternities as an archangel, glowing, calm and hard to read, and Narset suspected the change had taken as much from her as it gave. Through the story her stillness cracks, first at the sight of Ajani and at last in a fierce, grieving smile before she leaps at Sarkhan. {Elspeth, Storm Slayer} doubles every token its owner makes, and its zero ability gives her whole side wings for a turn, the way she flew alone at Sarkhan's horde. Ajani Goldmane is the leonin who could not forgive himself for what he did as [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn)'s creature, and who finally chooses to live and fight beside his friends.",
                        "Sarkhan Vol is the man whose rescue of [Ugin](who/ugin) created this Tarkir in the first place. He lost his spark and nearly his dragon shape, ate a dragon's heart to get them back, became a monster with every wild dragon at his command, and fled burned by ghostfire. {Sarkhan, Dragon Ascendant} shows him as a druid with a treasure and a dragon's longing, and his words on {Dracogenesis} and {Mox Jasper} show what he became.",
                        "Ugin, the spirit dragon, is the plane's oldest wound and its keeper. His slow healing fed the dragonstorms, his leaving let them run wild, and he spent his strength holding his brother [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) in a prison that broke. {Ugin, Eye of the Storms} exiles a coloured permanent the moment it is cast and again with every colourless spell after. Narset herself is the story's heart, a brilliant and awkward scholar who counts feathers in a crowd to calm her nerves, would rather have more bookshelves than ornaments, and in the end calls Elspeth and Ajani her friends.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Let the dragonstorms rage. Let what seeks sustenance feed. Let Tarkir be what it was always meant to be.",
                        "Sarkhan, Dracogenesis",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Episodes 1 to 7, by Cassandra Khaw, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-endure-flurry-renew", "Endure, Flurry, Renew",
                    listOf(
                        "Each clan got a new mechanic, and each one says something about the people who use it. The Abzan have endure, a keyword action. When a creature endures a number, its owner chooses either to put that many +1/+1 counters on it, or to create a white Spirit token of that size, the ancestors stepping out of the Kin-Tree to stand beside the living. It rewards the patient Abzan style of getting bigger and harder to kill. {Anafenza, Unyielding Lineage} endures whenever another creature its owner controls dies, and {Warden of the Grove} grows a little each turn and makes every new creature endure for as much as it carries.",
                        "The Jeskai have flurry, an ability word for something that happens whenever a player casts their second spell in a turn. It rewards casting spells in bunches, the way a monk strikes in combinations. {Cori-Steel Cutter} makes a monk token on every flurry and can move itself onto it, and it proved so strong in Standard that Wizards banned it there on June 30, 2025, in the largest Standard banning in twenty years. {Taigam, Master Opportunist} copies a second spell and puts the original on a slow suspend.",
                        "The Sultai have renew, an ability word on creatures that can be exiled from the graveyard, usually at sorcery speed, for one last effect, the body put to use even after death. {Qarsi Revenant} gives flying, deathtouch and lifelink counters to another creature, {Lasyd Prowler} turns lands in the graveyard into counters, and {Naga Fleshcrafter} turns a whole army into copies of one creature for a turn. Renew pairs with the set's counters theme, and some cards even care how many different kinds of counters are on a player's creatures.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-mobilize-harmonize", "Mobilize, Harmonize",
                    listOf(
                        "The Mardu have mobilize, a keyword with a number. When a creature with mobilize attacks, its owner creates that many 1/1 red Warrior tokens, tapped and attacking, and sacrifices them at the next end step, the horde riding in behind its leader for a single charge. It powers token and sacrifice decks alike. {Voice of Victory} mobilizes two and stops opponents casting spells during its owner's turn, {Avenger of the Fallen} mobilizes one Warrior for each creature card in its owner's graveyard, and {Zurgo, Thunder's Decree} lets warriors survive their end step.",
                        "The Temur have harmonize, a keyword on instants and sorceries. A card with harmonize can be cast again from the graveyard for its harmonize cost and then exiled, and the caster may tap one of their creatures to pay part of the cost equal to that creature's power, the whole pack singing together to make the spell happen. Big creatures make it cheap. {Winternight Stories} draws three again from the graveyard, {Nature's Rhythm} fetches a creature from the library twice, and {Songcrafter Mage} can give harmonize to any spell in the graveyard for a turn.",
                        "Taken together, the five mechanics read like the clans' own creeds. Endure is the Abzan belief that the ancestors stand beside the living, and renew is the Sultai conviction that nothing, not even a corpse, goes to waste. Flurry is the Jeskai Way of acting in disciplined combinations, mobilize is the Mardu horde that answers its khan's charge, and harmonize is the Temur pack lending its strength to one song. The khans' cards show them off, with {Felothar, Dawn of the Abzan}, {Narset, Jeskai Waymaster}, {Kotis, the Fangkeeper}, {Zurgo, Thunder's Decree} and {Eshki Dragonclaw} each rewarding the way their clan fights.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Hear me, Mardu! Our freedom is only beginning!",
                        "Zurgo, Thunder's Decree",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-dragon-mechanics", "Dragon Mechanics",
                    listOf(
                        "Dragons have rules of their own. Behold a Dragon is a new keyword action. To behold a dragon, a player either chooses a dragon they control or reveals a dragon card from their hand, and the set rewards them for having one ready. {Sarkhan, Dragon Ascendant} makes a treasure if his owner beholds a dragon when he enters, and a common cycle of breath spells such as {Molten Exhale} gets better with a dragon at hand, that one able to be cast at instant speed. Dragons appear at every rarity and in every colour, and the set's new {Mox Jasper} only taps for mana while its owner controls one.",
                        "Omen is a new spell subtype. Some dragons are printed as two cards in one, a dragon on one half and an omen instant or sorcery on the other. A player may cast either half, and when the omen resolves, the card is shuffled back into its owner's library rather than going to the graveyard, so the dragon is still there to find later. {Bloomvine Regent} can be cast instead as a land search, {Marang River Regent} as card draw, and {Scavenger Regent} as a sweeper that spares dragons.",
                        "A cycle of five uncommon dragonstorm enchantments do something when they enter, then return to their owner's hand whenever a dragon enters under that player's control, so they can be cast again and again. {Teeming Dragonstorm} makes two soldiers, {Encroaching Dragonstorm} fetches two basic lands, and red's {Breaching Dragonstorm} digs to the first spell in the library and may cast it for free. At the top sits {Dracogenesis}, which lets a player cast dragons without paying their mana cost.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "So, these dragons intend to also manifest through a sandstorm? I take some offense to that.",
                        "Felothar, khan of the Abzan, Teeming Dragonstorm",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-cycles", "Cycles",
                    listOf(
                        "The set is built around wedges, three colour combinations made of one colour and its two enemies, and its cycles lean into that. Five sieges, in each of the two colour pairs that two clans share, let a player choose one clan's ability as the enchantment enters. {Hollowmurk Siege} chooses between Sultai card draw and Abzan counters, and {Frostcliff Siege} between Jeskai card draw and Temur trample. Five sagas show each clan's beliefs and rituals, five rare legends show each khan, five mythic spells such as {Perennation} and {Jeskai Revelation} capture each clan's ideals, and five rare clan creatures such as {Fangkeeper's Familiar} round out the colours.",
                        "For mana, the set brought back the ten common gainlands and the five wedge triple lands such as {Sandsteppe Citadel}, both from the original Khans of Tarkir. Five rare lands like {Great Arashin City} come in untapped alongside their clan neighbours and have an ability of their own late in the game. Five colourless clan monuments such as {Abzan Monument} fetch a basic land and later turn into creatures, five common devotees such as {Mardu Devotee} fix mana, and the {Maelstrom of the Spirit Dragon} makes any colour for dragons and omens.",
                        "Five uncommon spells and five common creatures are costed in twobrid mana, three symbols of different colours each payable with either one coloured mana or two generic, so a clan card can be cast in a deck of only one or two of its colours, among them {Kin-Tree Severance}. The set also carries a few curiosities, among them {Tempest Hawk}, which a deck may hold any number of, and {Dragonstorm Forecaster}, which can only search for two specific cards by name.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm Release Notes, magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-cards", "Cards to Know",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-mythics", "Mythics",
                    listOf(
                        "Two planeswalkers lead the set. {Elspeth, Storm Slayer} doubles every token its owner creates, makes a soldier, buffs and grants flying to the whole team, or destroys a large creature, which makes it a natural centrepiece for any deck built on tokens. The spirit dragon [Ugin](who/ugin) gets {Ugin, Eye of the Storms}, which costs seven and exiles a coloured permanent the moment it is cast, then again with every colourless spell that follows, while drawing cards and making colourless mana. The set's headliner, {Mox Jasper}, costs nothing and taps for any colour of mana as long as its owner controls a dragon.",
                        "The five spirit dragons are all mythic, and so are the five clan spells. {All-Out Assault} gives the Mardu an extra combat with deathtouch, {Death Begets Life} destroys every creature and enchantment and draws a card for each, and {Dragonback Assault} clears the board then makes a flying dragon with every land. {Stormscale Scion} is a dragon with storm, copied for every spell cast before it that turn. {Clarion Conqueror} shuts down activated abilities of artifacts, creatures and planeswalkers for just three mana. {Call the Spirit Dragons} wins the game outright if five dragons of five colours can survive long enough.",
                        "Other mythics reward unusual decks. {Smile at Death} brings back two small creatures every upkeep. {Rot-Curse Rakshasa} spreads decayed counters from the graveyard. {Skirmish Rhino} drains for two on arrival. {Dracogenesis} makes every dragon free, and the set's reprint of {Craterhoof Behemoth} returned one of Commander's most famous finishers to the shelves.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Rage. Greed. Arrogance. People only accuse dragons of what they fear in themselves.",
                        "Sarkhan, Mox Jasper",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-standard-hits", "Constructed Hits",
                    listOf(
                        "The card that mattered most in Standard was {Cori-Steel Cutter}, a two mana equipment that turns every pair of spells into a monk with prowess. It became the engine of an Izzet prowess deck that dominated the format, and on June 30, 2025, Wizards banned it in Standard along with six other cards, the biggest Standard ban since 2005. Wizards said plainly that the Cutter was the engine of the deck. The set also reprinted {Snakeskin Veil}, a cheap green spell that protects a creature and gives it a counter.",
                        "Several dragons were built to matter outside the set's own draft. {Magmatic Hellkite} destroys a nonbasic land and leaves its owner a tapped basic that stays tapped a turn longer. {Clarion Conqueror} stops activated abilities of artifacts, creatures and planeswalkers on both sides for three mana, which hurts decks built on mana rocks and creatures that tap for effects. {Mox Jasper} is free acceleration for any deck that can keep a dragon on the table. The rare lands such as {Cori Mountain Monastery} and {Mistrise Village} give mono colour and two colour decks a useful land that comes in untapped when the right basic is around, and {Mistrise Village} can make a spell uncounterable.",
                        "{Voice of Victory} stops opponents casting spells during its owner's turn, a strong piece of disruption on a cheap body, and {Sage of the Skies} copies itself when it is the second spell of the turn. {Dragonfire Blade} costs one and gives a creature hexproof from single coloured sources, its equip cost dropping for every colour of the creature carrying it.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-commander-staples", "For Commander",
                    listOf(
                        "Commander players found plenty to love. {Elspeth, Storm Slayer} doubles tokens. {Herd Heirloom} ramps creature decks and gives big creatures trample and card draw. {The Sibsig Ceremony} makes creatures cheaper and then trades each one for a zombie, a strange engine for enter the battlefield effects. {Teval, Arbiter of Virtue} gives every spell delve. {Kotis, the Fangkeeper} casts an opponent's cards for free. {Betor, Kin to All} rewards the defenders and high toughness creatures of the Abzan with cards and, eventually, a halved life total for every opponent.",
                        "Dragon decks got the most. {Mox Jasper}, {Dracogenesis}, {Stormscale Scion}, {Maelstrom of the Spirit Dragon} and the dragonstorm enchantments all reward a deck built around them, and {Sarkhan, Dragon Ascendant} grows every time a dragon arrives. Between the five spirit dragons and the five khans, the set gave every wedge two new three colour legends to lead a deck, and the single coloured legends such as {Tersa Lightshatter} and {Surrak, Elusive Hunter} lead smaller decks of their own.",
                        "The set also brought back {Craterhoof Behemoth} and {Snakeskin Veil}, and it came with five Commander decks of its own, one per clan, each with new legends to lead them. Those decks have [their own tab](tdm-commander).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Banned and Restricted Announcement, June 30, 2025, magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-commander", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-the-decks", "The Decks",
                    listOf(
                        "Tarkir Dragonstorm came with five Commander decks, one for each clan, released the same day as the set under the code TDC. They are Abzan Armor in white, black and green, Jeskai Striker in blue, red and white, Sultai Arisen in black, green and blue, Mardu Surge in red, white and black, and Temur Roar in green, blue and red. Each held a hundred cards, ten double-sided tokens, a deck box and a small sample pack of Collector Booster cards.",
                        "Every deck carries two traditional foil legendary creatures with borderless art, one as the face commander and a second legend from the same clan beside it, and eight new rare cards in the normal frame. The new cards can be played in Commander, Legacy and Vintage, and reprints with the TDC code are legal wherever their name already is. Collector Boosters for the main set carried extended art versions of the new rares and non-foil versions of the borderless legends.",
                        "Each deck also has one of a new cycle of spells, the Will of the clans, such as {Will of the Abzan} and {Will of the Mardu}. Each offers a choice of two effects, and a player who controls their commander may choose both. Together the decks lean on the same theme as the set, clans and dragons, but each one plays very differently.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-temur-and-jeskai", "Temur and Jeskai",
                    listOf(
                        "Temur Roar is a dragon deck that ramps into a table full of them. Its commander, {Eshki, Temur's Roar}, grows with every creature spell, draws a card when that creature has power four or more, and when it has power six or more, deals damage equal to her own power to every opponent. Beside her stands {Ureni of the Unwritten}, who looks at the top eight cards when entering or attacking and puts a dragon onto the battlefield. The deck reprints old dragon names, among them {Dragonlord Atarka}, {Atarka, World Render} and {Sarkhan, Soul Aflame}, along with new spells like {Will of the Temur}.",
                        "Jeskai Striker plays noncreature spells. It is led by {Shiko and Narset, Unified}, the waymaster riding her spirit dragon, which copies the second spell of each turn if it targets and otherwise draws a card. {Elsha, Threefold Master}, a djinn monk, makes monk tokens as she deals combat damage. The deck also brings new cards like {Aligned Heart}, named for one of the three principles of the Way, and {Will of the Jeskai}.",
                        "Both decks echo their clans. Temur Roar is about the biggest creatures, the way the Temur honour strength and fight beside their dragons as family. Jeskai Striker strikes in combinations of spells and tokens, the way a monk fights.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-abzan-mardu-sultai", "Abzan, Mardu, Sultai",
                    listOf(
                        "Abzan Armor is built on walls and toughness. {Felothar the Steadfast} has every creature deal combat damage equal to its toughness, lets defenders attack, and turns a sacrificed creature into cards. Beside her is {Betor, Ancestor's Voice}, who grows creatures by the life gained and returns creatures from the graveyard by the life lost. The deck plays {Dragonlord Dromoka}, a long line of walls, and {Reunion of the House}, which brings back an army of creatures whose total power is ten or less, with a line of flavour text from Betor about standing or falling as a family.",
                        "Mardu Surge is tokens and sacrifice. {Zurgo Stormrender} mobilizes as he attacks, draws whenever an attacking token leaves and drains each opponent whenever a token leaves otherwise. {Neriv, Crackling Vanguard} makes two goblins as it enters and turns the variety of tokens into cards. The deck is full of token makers and sacrifice outlets, with {Will of the Mardu} making warriors equal to the creatures a player controls.",
                        "Sultai Arisen is a graveyard and land deck of zombies and druids. {Teval, the Balanced Scale} mills three as it attacks, returns a land to the battlefield, and makes a zombie druid whenever cards leave the graveyard. Beside it is {Kotis, Sibsig Champion}, who casts a creature from the graveyard once a turn by exiling three other cards. The deck plays {Teval's Judgment}, which draws, makes treasure or makes a zombie druid whenever cards leave the graveyard, and {Will of the Sultai}, which can return every land in the graveyard to the battlefield.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "Collecting Tarkir: Dragonstorm, magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm, Commander decks"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tdm-booster-fun", "Booster Fun",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-headliners", "Mox and Ghostfire",
                    listOf(
                        "The headline card of Tarkir Dragonstorm was {Mox Jasper}, a new mox that only works for a player who controls a dragon. In Collector Boosters it could appear as a serialized retro frame card in double rainbow foil, drawn by Dan Frazier, the artist of the original moxen of 1993. Serialized copies could turn up in Collector Boosters of any language but were always printed in English, and fewer than one Collector Booster in a hundred held one.",
                        "Ghostfire, the colourless fire of [Ugin](who/ugin), became a showcase frame on ten mythic rares. Ghostfire cards appeared only in Collector Boosters, in traditional foil about nine percent of the time and in halo foil about one percent of the time, a finish brought back for the set. In Japanese Collector Boosters they were always Japanese, and elsewhere they appeared in English two thirds of the time and in Japanese one third. {Ugin, Eye of the Storms} itself was among them.",
                        "Basic lands got two treatments. Full art dragon's presence lands, painted by Sergey Glushakov, show Tarkir's landscapes with the shadow of a dragon passing over them, and appeared in both kinds of booster. Dragon's eye lands, by Ron Spencer, show a single huge dragon eye up close, and appeared only in Collector Boosters, with a dragon's eye Wastes given out as a promo through store events.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-frames", "Frames",
                    listOf(
                        "Borderless reversible dragons have a cute illustration on one side and a fearsome one on the other, and five rare dragons and {Ugin, Eye of the Storms} were printed that way. Borderless clan cards set each card against a backdrop themed to its clan, fourteen mythics and thirty-six rares in all. The showcase draconic frame wraps cards in a dragon-themed frame with matching art, across nine mythics, five rares, fifteen uncommons and six commons, so even a common could look draconic.",
                        "Fifteen story-rich rares, the sagas, the sieges and some of the lands, got borderless art that tells their tale across the whole card, as did {Elspeth, Storm Slayer}. All of these appeared in both Play and Collector Boosters, in non-foil and traditional foil, and the Commander decks added their own borderless legends and extended art rares to the Collector Boosters.",
                        "The promos rounded it out. The buy a box card was an extended art foil {Qarsi Revenant}, the bundle promo was an alternate art foil {Temur Battlecrier}, and each prerelease pack added a year-stamped foil rare or mythic and a clan booster tied to the clan a player had picked.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "tdm-special-guests", "Special Guests",
                    listOf(
                        "Ten Special Guests cards, set code SPG, came with the set, reprints with new art that fit the plane. The headliners were the five enemy coloured fetch lands, {Arid Mesa}, {Marsh Flats}, {Misty Rainforest}, {Scalding Tarn} and {Verdant Catacombs}, each with a new Tarkir landscape. Non-foil copies replaced a common in about one and a half percent of Play Boosters, and traditional foils appeared in Collector Boosters.",
                        "In about one percent of Collector Boosters the fetch lands appeared in dragonscale foil, a new foil treatment, with art different again from the regular Special Guests versions. The other five Special Guests were the Ultimatums from Ikoria, one in each wedge, {Eerie Ultimatum}, {Inspired Ultimatum}, {Emergent Ultimatum}, {Ruinous Ultimatum} and {Genesis Ultimatum}, each newly painted by Dominik Mayer to show the spirit dragon of its clan.",
                        "Special Guests cards are legal in Commander, Legacy and Vintage, and anywhere else their names already are, and they could be opened and played in Tarkir Dragonstorm draft and sealed when they came from Play Boosters.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Story", "Collecting Tarkir: Dragonstorm, by Athena Froehlich and Jubilee Finnegan, magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
